A||N||4||stimpy||Firebird headlight motor troubles.||||9||
Z||000000||stimpy||11-01-2000||06:14 PM||||I replaced my drivers side headlight motor with a passenger side motor out of a Firebird. Problem is, the headlight will only come up about an inch before stoppping. If I lift up on the headlight door, or turn on the lights with the hood open, then the assembly works fine, goes all the way up.  I thought it might be the limit switch inside, so I replaced it with a switch out of a motor with a stripped gear, but still have the problem.  Any guesses what might be my issue? Do I just need to break down and buy some rebuilt motors from Doug?<BR><P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||9
Z||000001||StuGood||11-02-2000||12:00 AM||||Just an idea: Do you have fairly deep, worn grooves in the "rub strip" areas on the underside of the headlight door?  If so, might try bonding a smooth metal shim over the grooves, so the "ears" of the headlight frame don't sink in and drag.  <P>Better (?)idea: Think in the stock Firebird setup, there aren't any "sprung" doors in the hood, like Fieros have.  Could be the limit switch spring in a Firebird motor is weaker.  Might try substituting the spring from the old Fiero motor, and see how (if  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)it comes out.  The spring I'm referring to goes on the armature shaft, between the two little square blocks with fingers which actuate the limit swtiches.<P>Don't know if either of the above are valid, but something to try.  Please let us know what happens  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">    ||||reg||1
Z||000002||stimpy||11-02-2000||08:12 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by StuGood:<BR><B>  Might try substituting the spring from the old Fiero motor, and see how (if   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)it comes out.  The spring I'm referring to goes on the armature shaft, between the two little square blocks with fingers which actuate the limit swtiches.<P><BR>  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Yeah, Stu, that's the switch (for lack of better tech term) that I replacd out of an old Fiero motor. No good. Thanks for the response though.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||11-02-2000||12:58 PM||||Hmmm.... well, in case it makes you feel better, you're not alone.  I rebuilt a (yes - driver's side) HL motor for someone this last summer.  Before I messed with it, the HL wouldn't come up at all.  They were happy when I got it to come up with help (just like yours).  Same deal - with the hood open, the HL would come up all the way; otherwise (w/ hood shut), it came up an inch or so until you pulled it up.<P>Seems like either the drag from the groovy rub strips, or just the added load of the HL door springs must be enough to trip the HTS switch (duh).  That, or some kind of binding in the linkage at some point in its travel, that is exacerbated by the load of the door.<P>Wonder if those HTS springs pooh-out eventually, and lose their temper   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ? <BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 11-02-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000004||GarethWright||11-02-2000||06:30 PM||||What was happening on your original motor? Hit headlight switch, one light opens, the other stays down with the motor whirring?<BR>If So...<BR>On the 87-88 system, there is a bushing that wears out(translation, gets destroyed) in the headlight motor. You can get a rebuild kit with the bushings from Ecklers Corvette (mail order place out of Titusville, FL. Phone number: 1-800-327-4868) for about $5. The part number is E-29304. The Corvette headlight motors use the same bushing.<BR>If you take the gear end of the motor apart (don't take the Motor end completely apart, as the spring-loaded contacts are a pain to get back in) Inside there's large white plastic gear, with a metal cap that is attached to the arm that pushes the light assembly up.<BR>If you take the metal cap off of the plastic gear, likely you fill find lots of little plastic crumbs. Those are the remains of the 3 round bushings, Like 3 "O"s.  When the motor strains too hard on something, instead of stripping the large white gear, it just smashes those little round bushings. Its quite easy to do, it took me about 15 mins (after gettin the motor end back together    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) Hope that was Some help to Somebody    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Gareth<P>Oh yea, mine worked fine with the hood open, the bushing crumbs give enough grip to lift the headlight as long as theres no resistance (aka spring loaded headlight door)<P>[This message has been edited by GarethWright (edited 11-02-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by GarethWright (edited 11-03-2000).]||||reg||1
